# Guides

User guides, engineering guides, runnable how-tos. A guide is the *playbook*
for a rule whose imperative form lives elsewhere (CONVENTIONS §6–§18, an ADR,
the root `AGENT.md`): the rule says what must hold, the guide shows how to
make it hold, with worked examples. When a guide and its rule disagree, the
rule wins and the guide is the thing to fix.

Start with `dev-loops.md` (how work is done here) and `python-style.md` (how
code is written); reach for the rest when you are building the thing they name.

## What ships here

| Member | Purpose | Not for | Rule stated in |
|--------|---------|---------|----------------|
| `agent-runtimes.md` | Declare an in-process agent's control flow as a neutral `Plan` (`Step`/`Edge`/`END`, effects from the §10 `tool_effect` vocabulary) and run it on a `Runtime`; the dry-run effect-guard, checkpointing, `interrupt()`, fan-out and `to_mermaid()` all on the stdlib `inprocess` engine, with LangGraph as one lazily-loaded adapter opted into per agent | Making a service reachable or discoverable over a network — a card, `ask`, `health` and a wire dialect are `agent-surface.md`; nor the loop a human or LLM follows while editing this repo, which is `dev-loops.md` (here the loop is data a machine executes) | CONVENTIONS §16; ADR-0003; `runtimes/README.md` is the package map |
| `agent-surface.md` | Make a service that crosses a process boundary discoverable as an agent: implement the neutral `AgentSurface` (`card`/`ask`/`health`) from `src/backend/agent_surface/`, mount the AAD wire adapter from `api/rest_fastapi/aad/`, version the generated schema, declare auth without the secret | Ordering the steps inside an agent's brain (`agent-runtimes.md`); nor an in-process brain at all — one the host imports stays a function under `agents/` (CONVENTIONS §13) with no port and no descriptor. Discovering *other* agents is the consumer's side and is out of scope by design | CONVENTIONS §14; ADR-0002 |
| `coding-practices.md` | The catalogue of code-level practices from `config/practices.json`, each sorted onto the gate / advisory / doc tier by one test (can a rule decide it with no model?), with the mechanism that enforces it (ruff, mypy, `check_J`/`K`/`L`/`M`, `scripts/check_practices.py`), the domain profiles, and the `agents/practice_refactor/` agent that brings existing code toward one named practice | How to write the comment, docstring, or failure path that no rule can judge — that is `python-style.md`; nor what a check script proves, at which tier it runs, or how it is wired as a hook — that is `deterministic-checks.md` (this file says which *practice* a mechanism serves, not how the mechanism runs) | CONVENTIONS §15 (`practices.profiles`) and §18 (the advisory-never-gates line); the registry is `config/practices.json` |
| `deterministic-checks.md` | The catalogue of every deterministic check (the "template linter"): each script's purpose, tier (`error` / `error*` / `report`), interpreter, and how it is wired as a pre-commit, CI, or scheduled trigger; the A–S list of what `scripts/check_structure.py` proves; how to add a check. Its checks table is machine-read by `check_R` and mirrored by the showcase's Checks page | Which coding practice a gate serves, or why something is a gate rather than an advisory (`coding-practices.md`); what a `make advise` finding means for your code (`generic-solution.md` for the answer-key smell, `coding-practices.md` for practice smells); or *when* to run the gate while working (`dev-loops.md`) | CONVENTIONS §6 (enforcement) and §7 (doers in `scripts/`, thin triggers) |
| `dev-loops.md` | How a human or an LLM works here: vertical slices, the TDD red → green → refactor loop on the mirror test, the bounded convergence loop ("Ralph": a written plan, a pass cap, the worklist re-derived from the repo each pass, a commit per pass), and an e2e scenario for every user-facing flow — each pass ending at `make verify`, never at self-report | Judging whether the green fix you just made is a patch or a real fix (`generic-solution.md`, the sibling discipline); a loop a *program* runs unattended, which is a `Plan` on a `Runtime` (`agent-runtimes.md`); or what each gate actually proves (`deterministic-checks.md`) | CONVENTIONS §17; root `AGENT.md` (the imperative form) |
| `generic-solution.md` | Solve the class an eval, golden set, or failing case only samples: the eval is a sample not the spec, name the general rule before you special-case, derive don't hardcode (the answer-key trap), fix the generator not the golden; plus exactly what `scripts/check_generic.py` (`make advise`) catches and deliberately does not | The cadence of passes, pass caps, or test-first ordering (`dev-loops.md`); the tiering of practices or the `scripts/check_practices.py` smells (`coding-practices.md`); or the catalogue row that says `check_generic.py` is a `report` and never a gate (`deterministic-checks.md`) | CONVENTIONS §18; root `AGENT.md` |
| `python-style.md` | The canonical statement of how Python is written here — the judgment half above the gated floor: the priority order (readable, robust, fast enough), the `title:`/`summary:` module header the corpus reads, docstrings say what and comments say why, silence is the defect (absent vs broken, a check that writes is not a check, exit codes are the contract), plain over clever, how a code agent works, and the same contract from a one-off script to a full product | Which rule enforces what, at which tier, or how to enable a domain profile — that is `coding-practices.md` (this file names `check_O`/`check_E`/ruff only to mark where the gate stops and judgment starts); nor the process discipline of slices, TDD and pass caps (`dev-loops.md`) | Root `AGENT.md` ("Write Python to the house pattern"); ADR-0008; the doc-tier entries `readability-over-speed` and `absent-vs-broken` in `config/practices.json` |
{% if showcase %}| `showcase-site.md` | The bundled product demo: an Astro wiki over a thin FastAPI `/api/*` router over the `src/backend/showcase/` read model of `config/project.json` + `wiki/corpus.json`; how it stays synced front-to-back with no host baked in, the `llms.txt` agent front door, and the static GitHub Pages build. Copier prunes it (with the whole showcase) when `showcase=false` | Exposing *your* service as an agent — `llms.txt` is a read-only projection of the corpus, not an agent surface with `ask`/`health` (`agent-surface.md`); nor the definition of the checks its Checks page lists (`deterministic-checks.md`) | ADR-0007 (the declinable showcase); root `README.md` "Showcase demo" |
